diff --git a/docs/catalog-sync.md b/docs/catalog-sync.md
index 43b1164..47cef37 100644
--- a/docs/catalog-sync.md
+++ b/docs/catalog-sync.md
@@ -73,6 +73,26 @@ back. `NPM_CATALOG_OFFLINE=1` uses the snapshot without any request.
 Entries other than the verified 27 link to npm and the publisher repository
 and have no detail page here, so the export stays at its current page count.
 
+## Pigpen releases
+
+`/packages` also lists what the Pigpen repository has released, in two sources shown before the npm ones once they have
+entries: **Pigpen Packages** and **Pigpen Piglets**. `scripts/build-pigpen-catalog.mjs` runs in `pnpm generate` after the npm
+catalog and reads Pigpen's generated `index.json` (raw HTTPS from `catalog.config.json`: one request, no redirects, no
+credentials, 10 s, 2 MiB).
+
+| Source | Entry is listed when | Command on the card |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Pigpen Packages | its release tag `components/<name>/v<version>` is recorded in Pigpen | `pig install 'git:https://github.com/<repo>.git@components/<name>/v<version>#subdirectory=components%2F<name>'` |
+| Pigpen Piglets | a signed `piglet-release.json` receipt, verified in Pigpen against its pinned Ed25519 key, is committed | `pig piglet pull 'github:<repo>/<name>@<version>'`, with platforms and signing key id |
+
+Planned entries are counted but never listed. The site does not verify signatures; Pigpen does, and the site re-derives each
+command and URL from the entry's own name, version and repository and refuses the entry when the index says otherwise, so a
+hand-edited index cannot put another command on a card. The index is validated against Pigpen's schema
+(`data/pigpen-index.schema.json`; copy it from Pigpen when the schema changes). A failed fetch or an index that does not
+validate keeps the committed `data/pigpen-index.snapshot.json`, never a partial list. `PIGPEN_OFFLINE=1` uses the snapshot;
+`PIGPEN_INDEX_FILE=<file>` reads that index. `pnpm catalog:snapshot` refreshes the snapshot (review the diff). pi-in-go.dev
+hosts no downloads: Packages come from Git tags and Binaries from GitHub Releases.
+
 ## Historical ingestion design (not the public build)
 
 ## Two authorities, one static catalog
